+<!--
+  SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+
+  SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+-->
+
+# Swipe engine (vendored)
+
+The MIT-licensed swipe recognition engine of the
+[Swipe Keyboard](https://github.com/itsdestin/swipe-keyboard) project, vendored
+as an in-tree static library so that the keyboard builds offline and the
+distribution packages carry a fixed, reviewable copy.
+
+- Upstream commit: `f48099755cd1ebeb3f3a9e40f680a683ccc95b2b` (2026-10-24).
+- Copied: `engine/src/*.cpp` and `engine/include/swipekb/*.hpp`. The upstream
+  `CMakeLists.txt`, its tests and its plugin layer are not copied;
+  `CMakeLists.txt` here only builds the sources as `swipekb_engine`.
+- Upstream author: Destin Moss, https://youcoded.ai. Licence: MIT (see
+  `LICENSES/MIT.txt` in the repository root).
+- One local change: in `src/swipe_layout.cpp` the code-point table that maps a
+  letter to its key covers the whole Basic Multilingual Plane instead of
+  `0x180` (Latin-1 + Latin Extended-A). Without it every non-Latin letter
+  (Russian, Greek, Hebrew, Arabic) maps to "no key" and the vocabulary for
+  such a layout is built empty. The rest of the sources keep their SPDX
+  headers and are otherwise unchanged.
+
+## What it does
+
+`swipekb::LanguageEngine` is the entry point: it takes a finger path, the
+letter-key rectangles and the previous words, and returns ranked dictionary
+words. A word must be in the dictionary to be returned. With no swipe model it
+decodes by key geometry alone, which is what the Russian language uses; the
+English language also loads the optional 2.1 MB MIT neural model.
+
+The dictionary format is AOSP `.combined`; `tools/generate-swipe-dictionaries.py`
+builds it from the predictive word lists (`src/prediction/data/*.bin`) at build
+time.
+
+## Updating
+
+1. Copy the new `engine/src` and `engine/include/swipekb` over these files.
+2. Update the commit hash above.
+3. Run the keyboard's tests and `reuse lint`.
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/language_engine.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/language_engine.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..d7d337e14bff5812eb17aa8ec9e8c198c2d3d36b
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/language_engine.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// Integrators, start here (see engine/README.md for a worked example, the files to download and their licences):
+//   LanguageEngine e; LanguageEngineSetup s; s.language="en"; s.dictionary="en_swipe.combined";
+//   e.init(s);  e.recognize(trace, keys, contextWords(textBeforeCursor), 3);  e.complete("hel");
+// Only the dictionary is required; the n-gram pack and the swipe model are optional and each one that is missing
+// or damaged only switches its own part off. Needs Qt 6 Core. MIT licence.
+//
+// LanguageEngine — everything the keyboard (and the Gaming Mode daemon) asks of one language, behind one class:
+//   * swipe recognition: the project's own swipe engine (network + dictionary beam search + geometric cross-check,
+//     with the geometry-only decoder as fallback) ranked by the language's n-gram sentence prior when it has one;
+//   * the language's dictionary (`.combined` word lists): typed-word completion with one-edit corrections, similar
+//     words for tap-to-swap, other spellings (its / it's), the dictionary's display spelling, offensive flags;
+//   * words added at run time: the person's learned words (a set that is replaced, so forgotten words go away)
+//     and the built-in texting words (which only ever raise a word).
+//
+// WHY one facade: the plugin and the daemon must give the same words for the same input, and neither should know
+// which parts (model file, n-gram pack) are present. Nothing here throws to the caller: a missing or damaged
+// model or n-gram pack only removes that part, a broken swipe gives no guesses.
+//
+// Threading: one instance per thread; call every member from the thread that owns the instance.
+#pragma once
+
+#include <memory>
+#include <string>
+#include <utility>
+#include <vector>
+
+#include "swipekb/spellings.hpp"
+#include "swipekb/text.hpp"
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+struct LanguageEngineSetup {
+    std::string language = "en";   // "en", "es", "fr", "de"; German keeps the dictionary's capitals (nouns)
+    std::string dictionary;        // the language's verified `.combined` word list (required)
+    std::string ngram;             // optional n-gram pack ("SWKBNG2"): sentence prior for swipes; "" = word frequency
+    std::string swipeModel;        // optional swipe network file ("SKNN"); "" or unusable = geometry only
+};
+
+// How the guesses after the first one are chosen (recognize()). WHY its own knobs: the first guess is what the
+// finger path and the sentence say most strongly; the others are only worth showing when they are words the person
+// could have meant. Without these, the next-closest finger paths came out as rare names, stretched words and rare
+// words ("you" -> "youuuuu", "tou"; "way" -> "waaaay", "watt"). Defaults tuned on 2,235 recorded English swipes
+// from sessions never trained on, tuned on or scored with (2026-10-24): shown alternatives that are rare,
+// stretched or rare names fell from 43.7% to 5.5%; the right word in the top 3 went 93.38% -> 92.80% (those
+// recordings are word prompts, rich in rare words and names); the first guess is unchanged by construction.
+// Other languages keep only dropStretched (see init()): the frequency rules lowered their top 3.
+struct AlternativeParams {
+    int pool = 16;              // the swipe engine's best N words considered for the places after the first
+    float sentenceWeight = 1.f; // extra weight on the sentence/frequency prior when ordering those places
+    int minFrequency = 80;      // dictionary frequency (0..255) below which a word is never an alternative
+    int nameMinFrequency = 100; // the same for words the dictionary writes with a capital (names), except German
+    float margin = 1e9f;        // an alternative may score at most this far (nats) below the first guess (off)
+    bool dropStretched = true;  // drop words with one letter three times in a row ("youuuuu") as alternatives
+};
+
+class LanguageEngine {
+public:
+    LanguageEngine();
+    ~LanguageEngine();
+    LanguageEngine(const LanguageEngine &) = delete;
+    LanguageEngine &operator=(const LanguageEngine &) = delete;
+
+    // Reads the dictionary (required) and the optional parts. False (and not ready) when the dictionary cannot be
+    // read or holds no usable word.
+    bool init(const LanguageEngineSetup &setup);
+    bool ready() const;
+    bool hasModel() const;      // false = swipes are decoded by geometry only
+    bool usingNgram() const;    // true = the n-gram pack loaded and ranks swipes by the sentence
+    const char *mode() const;   // "own" (network + search) or "own-geometry"
+
+    // Best-first guesses (display spellings), at most topK. `keys` are the live letter-key rectangles; `context` is
+    // the previous words of the sentence, oldest first (contextWords()). Empty when not ready or the input is
+    // unusable. The first call after a new letter set or new words rebuilds the word list once (a short pause).
+    std::vector<Candidate> recognize(const std::vector<TracePoint> &trace, const std::vector<KeyRect> &keys,
+                                     const std::vector<std::string> &context, int topK);
+    // The guess of the last recognize() whose path fits the swipe best, before the sentence prior ("" if none).
+    std::string bestShapeWord() const;
+
+    // Dictionary words that start with the typed letters, most frequent first, followed by close corrections
+    // (one letter replaced, missing, extra or two swapped; a missing double letter costs least). Never a word
+    // shorter than what was typed. Empty for "" or more than 48 letters.
+    std::vector<Candidate> complete(const std::string &typed, int topK = 3) const;
+    // Whole dictionary words exactly one edit from `word`, most frequent first; never `word` itself, its other
+    // spellings or words that merely extend it. Case-insensitive. Empty for words under 2 or over 48 letters.
+    std::vector<Candidate> similar(const std::string &word, int topK = 12) const;
+    // The same swipe's other dictionary spellings, most frequent first ("its" -> {"it's"}).
+    std::vector<std::string> otherSpellings(const std::string &word) const;
+    // The dictionary's usual spelling of a word typed in any case or without its apostrophe ("dont" -> "don't");
+    // the word itself when the dictionary does not know it.
+    std::string displaySpelling(const std::string &word) const;
+    // True when the dictionary (or a learned/texting word) has this word; case, apostrophes and hyphens ignored.
+    bool hasWord(const std::string &word) const;
+    // True when the dictionary marks the word offensive (flags=offensive / possibly_offensive=true).
+    bool isOffensive(const std::string &word) const;
+
+    // The person's learned words (0..255 frequency). Replaces the previous set: words left out are gone again
+    // (a dictionary word gets its own frequency back). A learned frequency overrides the dictionary's.
+    // Words must be letters only (no digits, apostrophes or spaces); others are skipped.
+    void setLearnedWords(const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, int>> &words);
+    // Built-in texting words (lol, wyd, jaja...): only RAISE a word. A word the dictionary has at this frequency or
+    // higher keeps its own; a word it marks "not a word" comes in at this frequency. Same letters-only rule.
+    void liftWords(const std::vector<std::pair<std::string, int>> &words);
+    // Words shown with a capital letter (Capitals). Lower case stays the rule for every other word. Ignored in
+    // German, which keeps the dictionary's own capitals; nullptr = all lower case (as before).
+    void setCapitals(std::shared_ptr<const Capitals> capitals);
+    // Replaces the alternative-guess rules (see AlternativeParams); for measurement tools.
+    void setAlternativeParams(const AlternativeParams &params);
+    // The dictionary frequency (0..255) of this spelling (learned and texting words included); 0 = unknown.
+    int frequency(const std::string &word) const;
+
+private:
+    struct Impl;
+    std::unique_ptr<Impl> d_;
+};
+
+} // namespace swipekb
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/learning.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/learning.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..66accc12ae6ea95f0b608c93f8cddb63a55441ad
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/learning.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,162 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// PersonalModel — what the keyboard learns from the person's own typing (Phase 2 of the open-engine roadmap).
+//
+// It remembers three small things, all local and all per language:
+//   * words the person uses (new words such as names and slang, plus a use count for ordinary words),
+//   * word pairs ("previous word" -> "word"), and
+//   * words the person asked to forget (never suggested again until they type the word themselves).
+// It never sees the text of a field: the keyboard hands it single words that were typed or swiped, after the
+// same privacy gates that guard next-word prediction.
+//
+// WHY plain C++ with no Qt and no keyboard types: the offline personal test (tools/swipekb-personal-eval) must
+// run the exact code the keyboard runs, with no keyboard library. Callers lower-case words before
+// handing them in ("keys"), so the unicode rules stay in one place (languagetextrules in the plugin).
+// WHY single-threaded: only the keyboard's GUI thread touches it.
+//
+// WHY it re-ranks lists instead of changing the n-gram predictor: the predictors only return an ordered list
+// of words, and the personal layer sits beside them. A different predictor (e.g. a neural next-word model)
+// drops in underneath without touching this file.
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstddef>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <string>
+#include <unordered_map>
+#include <unordered_set>
+#include <vector>
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+struct LearningLimits {
+    // Caps keep the file small and the lookups fast; the least-recently/least-often used entries go first.
+    std::size_t maxWords = 4000, maxPairs = 12000, maxForgotten = 300;
+    // A use counts half as much after this many days without another use (the owner asked for 30-60 days).
+    double halfLifeDays = 45;
+    // A word the language does not know becomes suggestible after this many uses (owner decision: 2).
+    unsigned newWordUses = 2;
+};
+
+// How the person's own habits are mixed with the predictor's order. Each candidate gets a score that is a blend
+// of three probabilities that add up to one:
+//   (1 - a - b - n) * what the predictor's order says
+//     + a * how often THIS person put it after the previous word
+//     + b * how often THIS person writes it among words that start so
+//     + n * the same, among only the words the language files do not know (names, slang).
+// WHY the last one is separate: a word the language files lack cannot be in the predictor's list at all, so the
+// person's own use is the only evidence there is and deserves more trust than a nudge to an ordinary word.
+// The blend weights grow with the amount of personal evidence (a few uses count for little, many for more) and
+// are capped, so the predictor's choices are never thrown away. Tuned on the Tatoeba personal test.
+struct LearningWeights {
+    double rankRatio = 0.4;    // the predictor's n-th choice is worth rankRatio^n before normalising (each place is this much as likely)
+    double pairK = 8.0;        // pair evidence of N uses gets blend weight pairMax * N / (N + pairK)
+    double pairMax = 0.3;
+    unsigned pairMinUses = 2;  // a pair seen only once is a coincidence, not a habit, and counts for nothing
+    double wordK = 30.0;       // the same for "words I write that start with these letters"
+    double wordMax = 0.15;
+    double nextWordWord = 0.0; // 0..1: how much of wordMax applies when no letter is typed yet (a favourite word says little about the next one)
+    double novelK = 1.0;       // new-word evidence gets blend weight novelMax * (uses of new words) / (uses of all words with these letters + novelK); only once a letter is typed
+    double novelMax = 1.2;
+    double mixCap = 0.85;      // the personal weights together never exceed this
+};
+
+class PersonalModel {
+public:
+    explicit PersonalModel(LearningLimits limits = {}, LearningWeights weights = {})
+        : limits_(limits), weights_(weights) {}
+
+    // --- learning -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // What learn() changed, so a swipe the person immediately corrects or erases can be taken back.
+    struct Undo {
+        bool valid = false;
+        std::string prev, word;
+        bool hadWord = false, hadPair = false, wasForgotten = false;
+        double wordScore = 0, pairScore = 0;
+        std::int64_t wordLast = 0, pairLast = 0;
+        std::uint32_t wordUses = 0, wordCapUses = 0, pairUses = 0;
+        bool wordKnown = false;
+    };
+    // Counts one use of `word` (lower case) after `prev` (lower case, "" at a sentence start). `known` = the
+    // language's dictionary already has the word. `capitalized` = typed with a capital in the middle of a
+    // sentence (a name). `now` = seconds. Refuses (valid=false) words that are not plain single words.
+    Undo learn(const std::string &prev, const std::string &word, bool known, bool capitalized, std::int64_t now);
+    void undo(const Undo &undo);
+
+    // --- using what was learned ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    struct Ranked {
+        std::string key;      // lower-case word
+        int baseIndex = -1;   // place in the list given to rerank(), or -1 for a learned word added to it
+    };
+    // Re-orders `base` (lower-case keys, best first; may be empty) and returns at most `topK`. `prefix` = the
+    // letters typed so far of the current word ("" = next word). When `inject` is true, learned words that fit
+    // (follow `prev`, or start with `prefix`) join the list even if the predictor did not list them.
+    // Forgotten words are dropped from the result.
+    // `boostScale` scales the personal part (1 = full; swipes use less, because the finger path is strong evidence).
+    std::vector<Ranked> rerank(const std::vector<std::string> &base, const std::string &prev, const std::string &prefix,
+                               std::int64_t now, std::size_t topK, bool inject, double boostScale = 1.0) const;
+
+    bool isForgotten(const std::string &word) const { return forgotten_.count(word) != 0; }
+    // True when this person has used the word at least `minUses` times (a swear word they typed themselves
+    // may then be offered unprompted).
+    bool usedByPerson(const std::string &word, unsigned minUses = 2) const;
+    // True for words the language did not know and that now have enough uses to be suggested and swiped.
+    bool learnedNewWord(const std::string &word, std::int64_t now) const;
+    // True for a word the person taught the keyboard (the language files did not know it): only those can be removed one by one.
+    bool removable(const std::string &word) const;
+    // Name-like words (capital in the middle of sentences) are shown with a capital.
+    bool wantsCapital(const std::string &word) const;
+    // New words to add to the swipe dictionaries, with a 0..255 frequency. Changes only when the set changes.
+    std::vector<std::pair<std::string, int>> swipeWords(std::int64_t now) const;
+    std::uint64_t swipeWordsVersion() const { return swipeVersion_; }
+
+    // --- forgetting -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    void forget(const std::string &word);   // erases the word and every pair with it; a taught word is not suggested again until typed again
+    void clear();                           // forgets everything, including the forgotten list
+
+    std::size_t wordCount() const { return words_.size(); }
+    std::size_t pairCount() const { return pairs_.size(); }
+    bool empty() const { return words_.empty() && pairs_.empty() && forgotten_.empty(); }
+    bool dirty() const { return dirty_; }
+    void markClean() { dirty_ = false; }
+
+    // --- storage ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // Text with a version line and an end line. parse() returns false (and leaves the model empty) for anything
+    // damaged, truncated, from a newer version or oversized: a bad file must never break the keyboard.
+    std::string serialize() const;
+    bool parse(const std::string &text);
+
+    const LearningLimits &limits() const { return limits_; }
+    LearningWeights &weights() { return weights_; }
+
+private:
+    struct Word {
+        double score = 0;
+        std::int64_t last = 0;
+        std::uint32_t uses = 0, capUses = 0;
+        bool known = false;
+    };
+    struct Pair {
+        double score = 0;
+        std::int64_t last = 0;
+        std::uint32_t uses = 0;
+    };
+    double decayed(double score, std::int64_t last, std::int64_t now) const;
+    bool suggestible(const Word &w, std::int64_t now) const;
+    void prune(std::int64_t now);
+    void rebuildFollowers();
+    static std::string pairKey(const std::string &prev, const std::string &word) { return prev + '\x1f' + word; }
+    static bool plain(const std::string &s);
+
+    LearningLimits limits_;
+    LearningWeights weights_;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string, Word> words_;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string, Pair> pairs_;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::vector<std::string>> followers_;   // previous word -> words seen after it
+    std::unordered_set<std::string> forgotten_;
+    std::uint64_t swipeVersion_ = 1;
+    bool dirty_ = false;
+};
+
+}
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..6c74cbea0503222e326a14fa53b974fa98fe7cd9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,112 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// swipekb n-gram language model — "how likely is this word after the previous one or two words".
+//
+// WHY word-pair and word-triple statistics from a large text collection (a 3-gram model, scripts/lm/): they are
+// strong at exactly the cases that decide a swipe ("of the" vs "of thee", "I am" vs "I an"). The model is one read-only file, mapped into memory, so loading it
+// is instant and several keyboard processes share one copy.
+//
+// Plain C++ (no Qt types in the interface): the scoreboard tools and tests use this directly.
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstddef>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <string>
+#include <string_view>
+#include <unordered_map>
+#include <vector>
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+// Everything the model file holds, in plain vectors. Only the build tool (scripts/lm/ngram-build.cpp) and
+// tests fill one of these; the keyboard only reads the file.
+struct NgramModelData {
+    // Word number = index + 3. 0 = <unk> (any word not listed), 1 = <s> (sentence start), 2 = </s>.
+    std::vector<std::string> words;
+    // log10 P(word) and the back-off weight (log10) used when a bigram starting with this word is missing.
+    // Size words.size() + 3.
+    std::vector<float> uni_lp, uni_bo;
+    // Sorted keys (see NgramLM::key), with log10 P(w | v) and, for bigrams, the back-off weight (log10) used
+    // when a trigram starting with this pair is missing.
+    std::vector<uint64_t> bi_keys, tri_keys;
+    std::vector<float> bi_lp, bi_bo, tri_lp;
+    // Fallback for a word the model has never heard of (e.g. a dictionary swapped after the model was built):
+    // log10 P ~= calib_a + calib_b * f, where f is the dictionary's 0..255 frequency score, never below floor.
+    float calib_a = -7.f, calib_b = 0.f, floor = -9.f;
+};
+
+class NgramLM {
+public:
+    static constexpr uint32_t kUnk = 0, kBos = 1, kEos = 2;
+    static constexpr int kIdBits = 18;   // up to 262,143 words
+    static uint64_t key(uint32_t v, uint32_t w) { return (uint64_t(v) << kIdBits) | w; }
+    static uint64_t key(uint32_t u, uint32_t v, uint32_t w) { return (uint64_t(u) << (2 * kIdBits)) | key(v, w); }
+
+    // Lower case, letters only: the form a swipe spells (no apostrophes), and the form older models are built in.
+    // ASCII is lower-cased; other letters are kept as they are (English dictionary).
+    static std::string alphaForm(std::string_view word);
+    // Lower case, letters plus inner apostrophes (curly written straight): "We’re" -> "we're", "'tis" -> "tis".
+    static std::string apostropheForm(std::string_view word);
+    // The form THIS model's words are in: apostropheForm() for a model whose word list has apostrophes (built with
+    // scripts/lm/build-fineweb2-model.py --keep-apostrophes, so "were" and "we're" are different words), alphaForm()
+    // otherwise. WHY detected from the word list, not a new file version: older packs keep loading unchanged.
+    std::string form(std::string_view word) const { return apostrophes_ ? apostropheForm(word) : alphaForm(word); }
+    bool apostrophes() const { return apostrophes_; }
+
+    NgramLM() = default;
+    ~NgramLM();
+    NgramLM(const NgramLM &) = delete;
+    NgramLM &operator=(const NgramLM &) = delete;
+
+    // Maps the file. False (and stays unloaded) when missing, truncated or not a model file.
+    bool load(const std::string &path);
+    bool loaded() const { return base_ != nullptr; }
+    int order() const { return n_tri_ ? 3 : 2; }
+    size_t fileBytes() const { return size_; }
+
+    // Word number of a word in form(); kUnk when the model doesn't list it. In a model with apostrophes, a spelling
+    // without them that the model lacks ("dont") finds the model's most likely word with those letters ("don't").
+    uint32_t id(std::string_view word) const;
+
+    // The previous words, prepared once per swipe so scoring each guess is a couple of small lookups.
+    struct Context {
+        uint32_t u = kBos, v = kBos;   // the two previous words (u before v)
+        bool has_u = false;            // false for the first word of a sentence (only <s> before it)
+        uint32_t bi_lo = 0, bi_hi = 0;   // bigrams starting with v
+        uint32_t tri_lo = 0, tri_hi = 0; // trigrams starting with u v
+        float bo2 = 0, bo3 = 0;        // back-off weights (log10)
+    };
+    // `words` = swipekb::contextWords() output (oldest first, current sentence only); empty = sentence start.
+    Context context(const std::vector<std::string> &words) const;
+
+    // log10 P(word | context). `alpha` must be in form(). `dictFreq` = the dictionary's f score,
+    // only used if the model doesn't know the word.
+    float logProb(const Context &c, std::string_view alpha, float dictFreq) const;
+    float logProb(const Context &c, uint32_t w) const;
+    // Words the model has seen right after this context (trigram, then bigram continuations; may repeat).
+    // Every other known word scores max(floor, bo3 + bo2 + unigram), i.e. in unigram order.
+    void continuations(const Context &c, std::vector<uint32_t> &out) const;
+    float unigramLogProb(uint32_t w) const { return loaded() && w < vocab_ ? uni_lp_[w] : floor_; }
+    float floorLogProb() const { return floor_; }
+
+    // Writes `d` as a model file (atomically: temp file + rename). False on any I/O error.
+    static bool write(const std::string &path, const NgramModelData &d);
+
+private:
+    void unload();
+    const char *base_ = nullptr;
+    size_t size_ = 0;
+    uint32_t vocab_ = 0;
+    uint64_t n_bi_ = 0, n_tri_ = 0;
+    float calib_a_ = 0, calib_b_ = 0, floor_ = -9;
+    const float *uni_lp_ = nullptr, *uni_bo_ = nullptr;
+    const uint32_t *bi_off_ = nullptr, *bi_w_ = nullptr, *bi_tri_ = nullptr, *tri_w_ = nullptr;
+    const int16_t *bi_lp_ = nullptr, *bi_bo_ = nullptr, *tri_lp_ = nullptr;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string_view, uint32_t> ids_;
+    bool apostrophes_ = false;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string, uint32_t> alias_;   // letters only -> most likely word with those letters
+};
+
+} // namespace swipekb
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram_predictor.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram_predictor.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..c5e4f6c8cab8f53f104fc3fa3fcaea5630fe5683
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/ngram_predictor.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// Next-word suggestions from a language's n-gram pack (context.ngram + vocab.tsv): the Spanish, French and
+// German path of swipekb::Predictor.
+//
+// WHY its own file: the offline scorer (tools/swipekb-nextword-eval) must rank words with exactly the code the
+// keyboard runs, from a handful of sources.
+#pragma once
+
+#include <string>
+#include <unordered_map>
+#include <utility>
+#include <vector>
+
+#include "swipekb/ngram.hpp"
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+// The last 20 context words, each at most 256 bytes: the bound every prediction model sees.
+std::vector<std::string> boundedContext(const std::vector<std::string> &context);
+
+class NgramPredictor {
+public:
+    // False when the model is missing/damaged or the vocabulary is empty or unreadable.
+    bool load(const std::string &model, const std::string &vocab);
+    bool ready() const { return ngram_.loaded() && !vocabulary_.empty(); }
+    // Up to topK (max 64) display spellings, best first. May throw on allocation failure; callers catch.
+    // A non-empty prefix (the letters typed so far) keeps only words whose letters start with it: completion
+    // of a half-typed word, ranked by the same sentence context as the next word.
+    std::vector<std::string> predict(const std::vector<std::string> &context, int topK,
+                                     const std::string &prefix = {}) const;
+    const std::vector<std::string> &vocabulary() const { return vocabulary_; }
+
+    // For the hybrid with the neural model (nwp_predictor.cpp): the same ranking as predict(), but as
+    // (log10 score, vocabulary index) pairs, `want` of them (max 256), plus the context state so the neural
+    // side can score words the n-gram did not rank without recomputing it.
+    struct Ranked {
+        std::vector<std::pair<float, uint32_t>> top;
+        NgramLM::Context state;
+    };
+    Ranked rank(const std::vector<std::string> &context, size_t want, const std::string &prefix = {}) const;
+    float score(const NgramLM::Context &state, uint32_t vocabIndex) const;
+    // Letters-only lower-case form of a vocabulary word (what typed letters are matched against).
+    const std::string &alphaOf(uint32_t vocabIndex) const { return alpha_[vocabIndex]; }
+    const NgramLM &model() const { return ngram_; }
+
+private:
+    NgramLM ngram_;
+    std::vector<std::string> vocabulary_;
+    std::vector<uint32_t> wordIds_;            // model number per vocabulary word; kUnk when unknown
+    std::vector<std::string> alpha_;           // letters-only lower-case form of every vocabulary word
+    // WHY these three: next-word prediction scores only the words seen after the context plus the most
+    // frequent others, instead of all ~100-230k words (same ranking, many times faster; see predict()).
+    std::vector<uint32_t> byUnigram_;          // known-word indices, most frequent first, ties by spelling
+    std::vector<uint32_t> unknownBySpelling_;  // indices of words the model lacks, by spelling
+    std::unordered_map<uint32_t, std::vector<uint32_t>> indicesOfId_;
+};
+
+}
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_model.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_model.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..4462b4a98ce77a242e4e8474abb2944cc1138c22
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_model.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// swipekb next-word neural model (NWP LM): a one-layer LSTM with a projection and tied word vectors, the size
+// class of Google's published Gboard next-word models (arXiv 2305.18465). Plain C++ - no ML runtime.
+//
+// WHY this exists next to the n-gram: a 3-gram only sees the two previous words ("I have" -> any verb), the
+// LSTM carries the whole sentence in its state ("yesterday we ... " -> past tense, "she and her sisters" ->
+// plural). The file is produced by scripts/lm/nwp/export.py; its layout is documented there.
+//
+// One instance is single-threaded: step() and logits() reuse scratch buffers (the Predictor already lives
+// on one worker thread).
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstddef>
+#include <cstdint>
+#include <string>
+#include <string_view>
+#include <unordered_map>
+#include <vector>
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+class NwpModel {
+public:
+    static constexpr uint32_t kUnk = 0, kBos = 1, kEos = 2;   // token numbers; words start at 3
+
+    struct State {
+        std::vector<float> h, c;   // h: projected output (dim), c: cell (hidden)
+    };
+
+    // Reads and verifies the whole file (magic, version, exact size, checksum). False, and stays unloaded,
+    // when it is missing, truncated, damaged or from another version.
+    bool load(const std::string &path);
+    bool loaded() const { return vocab_ != 0; }
+    size_t fileBytes() const { return fileBytes_; }
+    uint32_t vocabSize() const { return vocab_; }     // including the three special tokens
+    uint32_t dim() const { return dim_; }
+    uint32_t hidden() const { return hidden_; }
+    // How much to trust the model against the n-gram, and the score bonus of words the model lacks (log10);
+    // tuned per language when the file is written.
+    float lambda() const { return lambda_; }
+    float oovBonus() const { return oovBonus_; }
+
+    // Token of a letters-only word; kUnk when the model does not list it.
+    uint32_t token(std::string_view alpha) const;
+    // The letters-only word of a token >= 3.
+    const std::string &word(uint32_t token) const { return words_[token - 3]; }
+
+    State start() const;                          // all zeros: nothing read yet
+    void step(State &s, uint32_t token) const;    // read one token (the input word)
+    // Scores of every token as the next one: out[t] = natural-log probability (log-softmax), size vocabSize().
+    void logProbs(const State &s, std::vector<float> &out) const;
+
+private:
+    struct Matrix {
+        const int8_t *q = nullptr;     // int8 rows (null when the file stores float)
+        const float *f = nullptr;      // float rows (null when int8)
+        const float *scale = nullptr;  // per-row scale for int8
+        uint32_t rows = 0, cols = 0;
+    };
+    void matvec(const Matrix &m, const float *x, float *out, bool add) const;
+    void embedRow(uint32_t token, float *out) const;
+
+    std::vector<uint8_t> blob_;
+    size_t fileBytes_ = 0;
+    uint32_t vocab_ = 0, dim_ = 0, hidden_ = 0;
+    float lambda_ = 0.5f, oovBonus_ = 0.f;
+    bool int8_ = false;
+    Matrix emb_, wih_, whh_, proj_;
+    const float *outBias_ = nullptr, *bias_ = nullptr;
+    std::vector<std::string> words_;
+    std::unordered_map<std::string_view, uint32_t> ids_;
+    mutable std::vector<float> gates_, x_, hfull_, hnew_;
+    mutable std::vector<int16_t> xq_;   // the vector being multiplied, rounded to whole numbers (int8 files)
+};
+
+} // namespace swipekb
diff --git a/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_predictor.hpp b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_predictor.hpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..0c80252ca5685f6263924f961d123b5978c50fbe
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/swipekb/include/swipekb/nwp_predictor.hpp
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+// SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Destin Moss <https://youcoded.ai>
+//
+// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
+
+// Next-word suggestions from a language's n-gram pack plus, when the pack has one, its neural model
+// (LANG.nwp.bin next to LANG.ngram): the path of swipekb::Predictor for Spanish, French, German and English.
+//
+// WHY a hybrid and not the neural model alone: the neural model knows the 24k most common words and sees the
+// whole sentence; the n-gram knows ~100k words and exact word pairs. Words are scored by both (log-linear
+// blend, weight stored in the model file); words the neural model lacks keep their n-gram score.
+//
+// Plain C++ interface: the scoreboard tools and tests use this directly. One instance is single-threaded
+// (it remembers the sentence state between calls, so typing one more letter or word is cheap).
+#pragma once
+
+#include <string>
+#include <unordered_map>
+#include <vector>
+
+#include "swipekb/ngram_predictor.hpp"
+#include "swipekb/nwp_model.hpp"
+
+namespace swipekb {
+
+class NwpPredictor {
+public:
+    // "x/es.ngram" -> "x/es.nwp.bin"; "x/context.ngram" -> "x/context.nwp.bin"; empty for any other name.
+    static std::string siblingPath(const std::string &ngramModel);
+
+    // The n-gram pack must load. `nwp` (default: siblingPath) is optional: absent or damaged = n-gram only.
+    // `siblingFallback=false` means "use exactly `nwp`, and no neural model when it is empty": the keyboard plugin
+    // passes only a file it has verified against the lock, so an unverified sibling must never be picked up.
+    bool load(const std::string &ngramModel, const std::string &vocab, const std::string &nwp = {}, bool siblingFallback = true);
+    bool ready() const { return ngram_.ready(); }
+    bool hasLm() const { return lm_.loaded(); }
+    const NgramPredictor &ngram() const { return ngram_; }
+    const NwpModel &lm() const { return lm_; }
+    // Overrides the blend stored in the model file (tuning and tests).
+    void setBlend(float lambda, float oovBonus) { lambda_ = lambda; oovBonus_ = oovBonus; }
+    void disableLm() { useLm_ = false; }
+    const std::vector<std::string> &vocabulary() const { return ngram_.vocabulary(); }
+
+    // Same contract as NgramPredictor::predict.
+    std::vector<std::string> predict(const std::vector<std::string> &context, int topK,
+                                     const std::string &prefix = {}) const;
+
+private:
+    NgramPredictor ngram_;
+    NwpModel lm_;
+    bool useLm_ = false;
+    float lambda_ = 0.5f, oovBonus_ = 0.f;
+    std::vector<int32_t> tokenToIndex_;    // neural token -> keyboard vocabulary index, -1 when the keyboard lacks it
+    std::vector<uint32_t> indexToToken_;   // keyboard vocabulary index -> neural token (kUnk when the model lacks it)
+    // Further keyboard words sharing a neural token (n-gram pack with apostrophes, neural model without: "were" and
+    // "we're" are one neural word). -1 ends the chain that starts at tokenToIndex_.
+    std::vector<int32_t> nextSameToken_;
+    bool lmApostrophes_ = false;           // the neural model's words have apostrophes (same form as the n-gram)
+    std::string lmForm(const std::string &word) const;
+    // Sentence state after reading cachedSeq_, and the next-word log-probabilities it gives.
+    mutable std::vector<uint32_t> cachedSeq_;
+    mutable NwpModel::State state_;
+    mutable std::vector<float> logp_;
+    mutable bool logpValid_ = false;
+};
+
+}
